import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { TimePickerHero } from "../../demos/time-picker/hero.rx"
import { TimePickerHourCycle } from "../../demos/time-picker/hour-cycle.rx"
import { TimePickerBounds } from "../../demos/time-picker/bounds.rx"
import { TimePickerList } from "../../demos/time-picker/list.rx"
import { TimePickerForm } from "../../demos/time-picker/form.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Time Picker" subtitle="A time field with a popup of hours, minutes and AM/PM to choose from.">
    <Demo source="time-picker/hero"><TimePickerHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      A time picker is a <a href="../time-field/">Time Field</a>, a trigger and a popup, behind one value. The field
      is Time Field's, so its segments are Time Field's parts, rendered by a small component inside it:
    </p>
    <Code file="time-picker/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      <code>{"<input type=\"time\">"}</code> is in every browser, but the <a href="../time-field/#platform">Time
      Field page</a> lists why it can't be the field: its locale and hour cycle are the browser's, its partial input
      can't be read, <code>step</code> can't take the minutes away, and Firefox can't style its segments. Its own
      picker is no better: it is different in each engine, and no engine lets it be styled.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the browser does do here is the popup. It is an element with the native <code>popover</code> attribute,
      opened by a <code>popovertarget</code> button, as in <a href="../popover/">Popover</a>: the top layer,
      closing on <kbd>Escape</kbd> and on a press outside, and placement against the trigger with CSS anchor
      positioning come from the browser. The field's hidden <code>{"<input type=\"time\">"}</code> submits the value
      and carries the native <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>required</code> checks.
    </p>
    <p>
      The library adds the columns (each a <a href="../listbox/">Listbox</a>, with roving focus and typeahead), the
      one shared <code>Temporal.PlainTime</code>, opening on the selected time, moving between columns, and
      disabling the options outside <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>. Base UI has no time picker, and no
      headless library has a canonical one: React Aria and Ark stop at the field.
    </p>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="choosing">Choosing a time</h3>
    <p>
      The value is committed as you go; there is no OK button. A choice in a column replaces that part of the time
      and shows in the field at once, and the popup stays open for the next one (<kbd>Space</kbd> or a click).{" "}
      <kbd>Enter</kbd> chooses and closes it; so do <kbd>Escape</kbd>, a press outside, and focus leaving the popup.
      Typing in the field moves the columns' selection, since both show the same value. While there is no value, a
      choice starts from <code>placeholderValue</code>, else from the time the popup opened at.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the Reminder demo, CSS lays out six options per row. Set <code>gridColumns</code> to match the row width
      so the arrow keys follow the layout. <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves between hours, AM/PM and minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the column layout, opening scrolls each column to its selected option and focuses the hours. With no value the columns open on
      the current time, rounded to the step. <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd> <kbd>Home</kbd> <kbd>End</kbd> and typing
      move within a column; <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> (mirrored when the popup is right to left) or{" "}
      <kbd>Tab</kbd> move between columns. Focus goes back to the
      trigger when the popup closes from the keyboard.
    </p>

    <h3 id="hour-cycle">Hour cycle and seconds</h3>
    <p>
      Put all four columns in the popup. The AM/PM column renders only in a 12-hour locale (or with{" "}
      <code>hourCycle={"{12}"}</code>), and the seconds only with <code>granularity="second"</code>. In 12-hour time
      the hours column lists the hours of the chosen day period. <code>minuteStep</code> and{" "}
      <code>secondStep</code> space the options.
    </p>
    <Demo source="time-picker/hour-cycle"><TimePickerHourCycle /></Demo>

    <h3 id="bounds">Minimum and maximum</h3>
    <p>
      Options outside <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> are disabled, not hidden: an hour when the whole hour is
      outside, a minute when the whole minute is. A choice is clamped into the range, so choosing 9 from 10:15 with
      a minimum of 9:30 gives 9:30. A time typed outside the range is kept, and the field is invalid.
    </p>
    <p>
      The step shapes the lists, not the value: 9:07 typed with a <code>minuteStep</code> of 15 is valid, and no
      minute is selected.
    </p>
    <Demo source="time-picker/bounds"><TimePickerBounds /></Demo>

    <h3 id="list">One list of times</h3>
    <p>
      Scheduling UIs show whole times at an interval instead of columns. <code>TimePickerOptions</code> is that
      list, every <code>interval</code> minutes from midnight, on the same root; the options outside{" "}
      <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> are disabled. Any choice sets the whole time and closes the popup.
    </p>
    <Demo source="time-picker/list"><TimePickerList /></Demo>

    <h3 id="form">In a form</h3>
    <p>
      With a <code>name</code>, or inside a named <code>FieldRoot</code>, the field's hidden input submits{" "}
      <code>13:45</code> (<code>13:45:00</code> down to seconds), and nothing while the field is incomplete. It is
      the only one: the columns submit nothing. A form reset puts <code>defaultValue</code> back.
    </p>
    <Demo source="time-picker/form"><TimePickerForm /></Demo>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>value</code> with <code>onValueChange</code>, and <code>open</code> with{" "}
      <code>onOpenChange</code>, to hold either yourself; <code>null</code> is a controlled, empty value.{" "}
      <code>onValueChange</code> gets a time for each choice and for each complete time typed, and{" "}
      <code>undefined</code> when the field becomes incomplete. Both callbacks run in a change of their own, so they
      may save without holding the picker. Disabled or read-only, the trigger is a disabled button.
    </p>

    <h3 id="styling">Styling</h3>
    <p>
      The parts ship no styles. Style the popup with <code>:popover-open</code> and <code>@starting-style</code>, not
      with a <code>display</code> rule on the popup itself (it would show the closed popover in place). The options
      are a listbox's: select them through the column, with <code>[data-selected]</code>,{" "}
      <code>[data-disabled]</code> and <code>:focus-visible</code>, as the demos do.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="time-picker" />
  </DocsPage>
}
